e  based on the evidence in the record a  reasonable observer would conclude that  the proclamation was motivated by  anti-muslim animus the majority holds  otherwise by ignoring the facts  misconstruing our legal precedent and  turning a blind eye to the pain and  suffering the proclamation inflicts upon  countless families and individuals this  is really a sad day for America make no  mistake the Supreme Court has endorsed  bigotry and Islamophobia the majority  and descent also sparred over  comparisons to Korematsu a 1944 ruling  upholding a presidential directive  ordering japanese-americans into  internment camps in World War two  justice Sotomayor claiming the  majority's reasoning today paralleled  that decision for more than seven  decades ago writing in Korematsu the  court gave a pass to an odious gravely  injurious racial classification  authorized by an executive order the  Chief Justice explicitly rejected the  comparison quote whatever rhetorical  advantage the dissent may see in doing  so Korematsu has nothing to do with this  case it is wholly an apt to liken that  morally repugnant order to a facially  neutral policy denying certain foreign  nationals the privilege of admission and  the case doesn't end here because the  Supreme Court was specifically ruling on  whether or not an injunction against the  travel ban 3.0 could stand so now today  with this guidance from the justices it  goes back to the lower court for a  decision actually on the merits Bret  Chen there was also another ruling today  regarding crisis pregnancy centers in  California how did that pan out well the  state there passed a law that they  managed to scoop up all the pro-life  pregnancy centers almost all of them in  the state essentially telling them  they had to provide all kinds of  information to potential clients  including about where they could find  abortions and access them well these  clinics sued saying that violates their  free speech rights and said it's  actually in antithetical to the mission  that they have which is to encourage  women to carry their pregnancies to term  it was a 5-4 decision written by  Clarence Thomas he essentially found  that it's a good chance these clinics  are going to prevail on the merits so he  sent back the case to California for  them to deal with that crap still  waiting on one more we are tomorrow  we're waiting on a big case involving  whether or not public sector employees  can be forced to pay fees or dues into a  union whose political messages they  don't agree with it could impact  millions of people across the country  we'll get it at 10 a.m. tomorrow morning  another big day shannon bream outside  the court Shannon thanks
 

